WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.954
Sustainable OT security depends on accurate operational practices.

00:00:04.954 --> 00:00:15.645
Asset inventory should record hardware, software, firmware, network identity, physical location, owner, vendor, function, criticality, communication, and support status.

00:00:15.645 --> 00:00:24.250
Inventory changes as equipment is replaced, temporary systems are added, and vendors modify support paths.

00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:32.854
Configuration management establishes trusted baselines for controllers, network devices, servers, workstations, applications, and security controls.

00:00:32.854 --> 00:00:39.373
Back up logic and settings, review differences, and document authorized changes.

00:00:39.373 --> 00:00:46.413
Change control should define testing, approvals, maintenance windows, rollback, and post-change validation.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:50.325
Patch and vulnerability management require coordination.

00:00:50.325 --> 00:00:59.972
Monitor vendor advisories, confirm affected assets and versions, evaluate exposure and consequence, test updates, and schedule work.

00:00:59.972 --> 00:01:07.012
When remediation is delayed, use compensating controls with owners and review dates.

00:01:07.012 --> 00:01:12.488
Unsupported technology needs lifecycle planning rather than indefinite exception.

00:01:12.488 --> 00:01:26.308
Logging and monitoring should focus on useful events: remote sessions, authentication, privilege, configuration transfer, new devices, boundary traffic, security-tool status, and changes to expected communication.

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:32.827
Analysts need operational context to distinguish approved maintenance from suspicious activity.

00:01:32.827 --> 00:01:40.388
Training should prepare operators, engineers, administrators, vendors, and incident responders for their responsibilities.

00:01:40.388 --> 00:01:46.907
Exercises can test loss of visibility, ransomware, remote-access misuse, and recovery.

00:01:46.907 --> 00:01:52.383
Lessons should update procedures, architecture, contacts, and control priorities.

00:01:52.383 --> 00:02:00.466
Continuous improvement uses evidence from incidents, near misses, assessments, exceptions, maintenance, and technology changes.

00:02:00.466 --> 00:02:09.592
Track whether controls work, whether risk decisions remain valid, and whether recovery can meet operational needs.

00:02:09.592 --> 00:02:18.718
Security becomes part of normal operations when ownership, maintenance, monitoring, and engineering decisions reinforce one another.
